.Dd November 8, 2026
.Dt NIGHTSAVER-COMMAND 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nightsaver-command
.Nd control a running nightsaver or xscreensaver daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl display Ar host:display.screen
.Op Fl quiet | Fl verbose
.Fl activate | deactivate | cycle | next | prev | lock | suspend | exit | restart | time | watch | version
.Nm
.Op Fl display Ar host:display.screen
.Op Fl quiet | Fl verbose
.Fl select Ar N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends commands to a running
.Xr nightsaver 1
daemon, and also to an
.Xr xscreensaver 1
daemon, using the xscreensaver control protocol.
Its options and messages follow
.Xr xscreensaver-command 1 .
Options may be given with one or two dashes and may be abbreviated to any
unambiguous prefix.
Exactly one command is accepted.
.Pp
The daemon is found through the
.Dq _SCREENSAVER_VERSION
property on the root window of the display, and the reply is read back through
.Dq _SCREENSAVER_RESPONSE .
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl display Ar dpy
The X display to talk to.
The default is
.Ev DISPLAY .
.It Fl quiet
Print output only if an error occurs.
.It Fl verbose
The opposite of
.Fl quiet .
This is the default.
.It Fl activate
Blank the screen now, as if the user had been idle.
Fails if the screen is already blanked.
.It Fl deactivate
Unblank the screen, as if there had been user activity.
If the screen is locked, it is not unlocked: the unlock dialog is shown.
When the screen is not blanked the idle timer is reset.
.It Fl cycle
If the screen is blanked, switch to another pattern.
Fails if the screen is not blanked.
.It Fl next
Like
.Fl activate
or
.Fl cycle ,
but run the next pattern in the list.
.It Fl prev
Like
.Fl next ,
in the other direction.
.It Fl select Ar N
Like
.Fl activate ,
but run pattern number
.Ar N
of the configured pattern list (1-based).
Fails if there is no such pattern.
.It Fl lock
Lock the screen now, blanking it first if necessary.
Fails if the screen is already locked.
Works even when locking is not enabled in the configuration.
.It Fl suspend
Like
.Fl activate ,
but power the monitor off immediately (DPMS).
.It Fl exit
Make the daemon exit.
Refused while the screen is locked.
Succeeds silently if no daemon is running.
.It Fl restart
Make the daemon re-execute itself.
Refused while the screen is locked.
.It Fl time
Print the version line and when the screen was last blanked or unblanked.
.It Fl watch
Print a line each time the saver state changes.
This command never returns.
.It Fl version
Print the name and version of the running daemon.
.It Fl help
Print a usage summary.
.El
.Pp
The
.Xr xscreensaver-command 1
option
.Fl demo
is parsed but the daemon answers that it is not supported.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width DISPLAY
.It Ev DISPLAY
The default display.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It 0
The command was accepted by the daemon, or
.Fl exit
was given and no daemon is running.
.It 1
A usage error, or the display could not be opened.
.It 255
The daemon refused the command (for example
.Fl activate
when already blanked,
.Fl exit
while locked), no daemon is running, there was no or an unrecognised
response, or the status property is missing or malformed.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Blank the screen, then lock it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nightsaver-command -activate
nightsaver-command -lock
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nightsaver 1 ,
.Xr xscreensaver 1 ,
.Xr xscreensaver-command 1 ,
.Xr nightsaver.toml 5
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Lucas Holt
